n canceled and the  parade will be virtual  senator roy blunt chairs the  congressional inauguration committee  inauguration of the president of the  united states  i think we're going to have somewhere in  the neighborhood of 2500 to 3 000 people  at the inauguration as opposed to 200  000  following the capital riot security is  being beefed up at least 20 000 national  guardsmen were sent to washington dc  ahead of the inauguration  that's more than twice the number sent  for president obama's inauguration when  security concerns were also high  both president-elect biden and i and his  team  are in agreement that uh events  of the attack on the capital make it  even more important  uh that we're outside we're at the place  where people are used to seeing the  inauguration of the president  one president who won't be attending is  president trump who with a week  remaining in his term became the first  president to be impeached for a second  time  after he was accused of encouraging a  mob to storm the capital in an effort to  overturn the election  there's going to be an air of tension  the question of whether democrats and  republicans  having just been through this trauma at  the capitol together  will look as if they're unified or not  will be a question hanging over an event  in which people always put on a face of  unity will that be the case this time or  not  like many presidents before him biden  will enter office at a time of  heightened national drama  hi gerald are poor do solemnly swear  but some of the most dramatic times have  seen some of the most memorable  inaugural addresses in history  jump back to 1865. lincoln's  second inaugural that occurred during  the civil war produced probably the  greatest  inaugural speech of all he said to the  country  it is time to bind our wounds we must  heal  and it was exactly the message the  country needed to hear  at that time it's also one that stood  the test of time  nearly 70 years later the u.s in the 